    "description": "A fast racerunner of the southeast — subspecies caucasia; COI may mark a separate species.",
    "overview": "The rapid racerunner (Eremias velox) is a lacertid. Tarkhnishvili et al. 2026 list it as biologically confirmed, with a broad range in the Caucasus and Central Asia. It is fairly common in southeast Georgia. The subspecies is E. v. caucasia; COI differences suggest caucasica may be a species separate from E. velox — infraspecific taxonomy needs population-level study. The figure is from Vashlovani National Park; barcodes are from Pantishara Canyon. It is harmless to people.",
    "habitat": "Southeast Georgia. The figure is from Vashlovani; barcoded specimens are from Pantishara Canyon.",
    "diet": "Small invertebrates; the checklist does not specify diet.",
    "behavior": "A diurnal, fast lizard of open ground. It is distinct from the sympatric steppe-runner; colour alone is not enough.",
    "conservation": "Biologically confirmed in the checklist. The discussion notes a deep split between Georgia/Caucasus material and E. velox from eastern China — a revision may follow, but this page does not split the species. No global IUCN category is given here.",
