    "facts": [],
    "identification": {
      "summary": "A small, smooth-skinned treefrog with sticky pads on the toe tips. In Georgia it is most often confused with the yellow-lemon treefrog; place is often more reliable than colour.",
      "traits": [
        "The body is short and the skin is smooth. Rounded sticky pads on the toe tips separate a treefrog from marsh frogs and toads. Adults are usually about 3–5 cm from snout to vent.",
        "The back is usually green, but it can turn grey or brown. Colour changes with temperature and background. The belly is usually pale — white or yellowish. A dark side stripe often forms a loop (hip sinus) at the groin; that stripe alone is unreliable, because some eastern treefrogs lack the loop and some yellow-lemon treefrogs show one.",
        "The eyes are large and the pupil is horizontal. The eardrum is visible and smaller than the eye. Males have a vocal sac on the throat. These marks help, but they are not enough on their own to split the two Georgian treefrogs.",
        "The yellow-lemon treefrog (Hyla savignyi) is the same genus. It lives in drier, less forested country south-east of Tbilisi. The eastern treefrog occupies most of the rest of Georgia — woodland and more humid ground. The breeding call differs. In the contact zone, including around Telavi, appearance can be intermediate.",
        "The marsh frog (Pelophylax ridibundus) is larger, stays in water and lacks sticky toe pads. Toads have warty skin and do not climb like treefrogs."
      ]
    },
    "faq": [
      {
        "question": "Does the eastern treefrog occur in Georgia?",
        "answer": "Yes. The 2026 checklist treats it as a species. DNA samples are from Tbilisi and Tskordza. The figure is Gaghma Dvabzu."
      },
      {
        "question": "Where can it be found in Georgia?",
        "answer": "In most regions — from Colchis through Kartli to the more humid parts of Kakheti. Do not expect it in the Vashlovani steppe or the dry Iori valley; that is yellow-lemon treefrog country."
      },
      {
        "question": "Is it venomous or dangerous to people?",
        "answer": "No. It has no venom and is harmless to people. Do not pick it up. Wash your hands after any contact."
      },
      {
        "question": "How can it be identified?",
        "answer": "A small green or grey frog with smooth skin and sticky pads on the toes. In Georgia, place matters: this is the woodland and humid-country treefrog."
      },
      {
        "question": "How does it differ from the yellow-lemon treefrog?",
        "answer": "Both are treefrogs. Colour is not reliable. Range is more useful: this species occupies most of Georgia; the yellow-lemon treefrog is the dry country south-east of Tbilisi. In the contact zone they can look alike."
      },
      {
        "question": "How large does it grow?",
        "answer": "Adults are usually about 3–5 cm from snout to vent. Separate Georgian measurements are not given on this page."
      },
      {
        "question": "What does it eat?",
        "answer": "Adults take invertebrates, mainly insects. There is no separate Georgian diet study on this page. Tadpoles eat algae."
      },
      {
        "question": "Why is it a candidate species?",
        "answer": "The checklist lists it as a separate species, but the split from H. arborea has not been checked enough with multilocus genomic data. That is a taxonomic note; it does not mean the frog is rare in Georgia."
      },
      {
        "question": "What should I do if I find one?",
        "answer": "Watch it and leave it where it is. Do not pick it up. It is not a medical risk to people."
      }
    ],
