    "facts": [
      "Described by Linnaeus in 1758. Order Carnivora, family Felidae, genus Panthera. Caucasus and Iranian Plateau populations are treated as one subspecies, P. p. tulliana (Valenciennes, 1856), following Kitchener et al. 2017 and IUCN. Synonyms include P. p. ciscaucasica (Satunin, 1914) and P. p. saxicolor Pocock, 1927. Do not split field sightings among those names.",
      "Georgian names in use are ჯიქი, კავკასიური ჯიქი, ლეოპარდი, and წინააზიური ჯიქი. English names include Caucasian leopard, Persian leopard, and Anatolian leopard. IUCN lists Georgian names as Jiki and Leopardi.",
      "The 2010–2014 action plan gives 1986 as the last recorded killing; some secondary sources give 1954. The dates disagree — both are reported here.",
      "Tracks were found in Vashlovani in autumn 2003, then a young male on camera; the last Vashlovani record in the action plan and CMS briefing is 2009. In August 2021 NACRES/WWF photographed one in Tusheti. In September 2025 the Agency of Wildlife published the Algeti camera-trap. WWF/Mongabay link that record to a male named Aren; the agency infers a longer presence on Trialeti. The versions do not fully match. Those three modern foci do not mean the leopard occurs throughout Georgia.",
      "The agency notes that in the Middle Ages the species was more widespread, and that older Georgian ვეფხვი could mean leopard. That is a historical name, not evidence that tiger (Panthera tigris) occurs in Georgia today."
    ],
    "identification": {
      "summary": "A large cat with a long tail, short ears without tufts, and a coat of rosettes — not a zigzag and not solid spots all over. In Georgia it is a rare vagrant.",
      "traits": [
        "In a measured Iranian series (Farhadinia et al. 2014; then treated as P. p. saxicolor) adult males often have a total length of 200–240 cm (mean about 221 cm) and mass 40–91 kg (mean about 66 kg); females are smaller — often 182–228 cm and 26–60 kg. There is no separate wild Georgian measurement series on this page. Ears are short and rounded, without tufts. The tail is about two-thirds of body length and darker toward the tip.",
        "Ground colour runs from yellowish-grey to golden-brown. Spots are rosettes: dark rings with a paler centre, not solid black dots over the whole body. The belly is paler. Cubs are darker and more heavily marked. Museum mounts in Tbilisi show historical specimens, not a living wild animal.",
        "Eurasian lynx (Lynx lynx) occurs in Georgia: it is much smaller, has ear tufts, and a short tail with a black tip. The leopard has a long tail and rosettes. Grey wolf (Canis lupus) is taller and not spotted. Snow leopard (Panthera uncia) does not occur in Georgia. This atlas has no lynx page yet.",
        "Live photos on this page are captive or zoo animals, not wild Georgian frames. Wild Georgian records are mainly camera-trap images released by organisations."
      ]
    },
    "faq": [
      {
        "question": "Is the leopard found in Georgia?",
        "answer": "Yes, but rarely. IUCN lists the country as extant and vagrant. The national Red List status is CR. Modern confirmation is by camera-trap in Vashlovani, Tusheti, and Algeti. The 2025 Algeti record was published by the Agency of Wildlife; it is not a countrywide density."
      },
      {
        "question": "Where can I see it in Georgia?",
        "answer": "Realistically, almost nowhere with any guarantee. Named sites are Vashlovani, Tusheti, and Algeti. There is no tourist “almost guaranteed” sighting. This page does not colour the regional map."
      },
      {
        "question": "How can it be identified?",
        "answer": "A large cat with rosettes, a long tail, and no ear tufts. Lynx has a short tail, ear tufts, and is much smaller."
      },
      {
        "question": "How large does it grow?",
        "answer": "In the Iranian series, adult males often measure 200–240 cm total length and 40–91 kg; females are smaller. There is no wild Georgian series here."
      },
      {
        "question": "What does it eat?",
        "answer": "Mainly medium and large ungulates. Diet has not been measured separately in Georgia. Livestock may be taken if wild prey is scarce."
      },
      {
        "question": "When is it active?",
        "answer": "Mainly at night and at dusk. It does not migrate like many birds; individuals may move across borders."
      },
      {
        "question": "Is it dangerous?",
        "answer": "It is not venomous. It is a large predator. Do not approach. A series of attacks on people in Georgia is not documented on this page."
      },
      {
        "question": "Is it protected?",
        "answer": "Yes. It is CR on the Georgian Red List, hunting is prohibited, and it is on CITES Appendix I. The subspecies is Endangered globally; the species is Vulnerable."
      }
    ],
