    "facts": [
      "Linnaeus described it in 1758. The accepted name is Sus scrofa. Order Artiodactyla (even-toed ungulates), family Suidae, genus Sus. It is not a ruminant.",
      "Subspecies counts are disputed (roughly 4 to 16 or more, depending on the author). The Georgian encyclopaedia assigns Georgia to Sus scrofa attila. The IUCN Wild Pig Specialist Group profile limits attila to the northern flank of the Caucasus and excludes Transcaucasia (Georgia, Armenia, Azerbaijan); Groves notes that pigs north and south of the Caucasus differ sharply and that Transcaucasian boars are not attila. Do not assign a Georgian individual to a subspecies in the field with confidence.",
      "In Georgian the usual name is გარეული ღორი; the male is often ტახი, the female ნეზვი, the young გოჭი. In English: wild boar, Eurasian wild pig, wild pig. The domestic pig is often written Sus scrofa domesticus or Sus domesticus — a domesticated form of the same species.",
      "IUCN lists Georgia as native. It is in Bukhnikashvili and Kandaurov’s 2002 annotated mammal list. It is on the hunting-species list. That is country-level confirmation, not a regional map."
    ],
    "identification": {
      "summary": "A large even-toed ungulate with a short neck, a long snout, coarse bristles, and a straight back line. Males often show upward-curving tusks. It is resident in Georgia.",
      "traits": [
        "At species level head–body is about 90–200 cm, tail 15–40 cm, shoulder height 55–110 cm, mass often 44–320 kg. Females are usually about 80% the size of males. There is no separate measured Georgian series on this page. The Georgian encyclopaedia gives males to 2 m body length, 130 cm at the shoulder, and 50–300 kg; those are maxima in a secondary source, not a sample measured here. The snout is long, the ears erect, the eyes small.",
        "The coat is coarse bristle, often with a softer underwool. Colour runs from brown to almost black and often greyer with age. In males the upper canines curve out and up; the lower canines stay sharp by rubbing. In females the tusks are smaller and often hidden. Piglets have longitudinal pale stripes on a brown ground — a reliable age mark.",
        "Georgia has no other wild suid. The domestic pig is the same species and hybridises freely; a “pure wild” animal and an escaped domestic pig cannot always be separated in the field. Roe deer (Capreolus capreolus) is slimmer, longer-legged, and has antlers in males, not tusks. Brown bear (Ursus arctos) is much larger and leaves a plantigrade print, not a cloven hoof.",
        "It is not venomous. The risk is physical: a charge and tusks. Do not approach a sow with piglets. Do not handle it."
      ]
    },
